Decision rule:

```text
if blocked_codex_tickets_in_project_scope >= 1:
    transition the highest-priority/current blocked card only to In Progress
    resume its original worker or dispatch one continuity replacement
elif current_codex_card_is_triage_due_to_block_loop_detected:
    recover only that same card to In Progress
    resume its original worker or dispatch one continuity replacement
elif ready_or_running_codex_tickets_in_project_scope == 0:
    report no Kanban-backed `[Codex]` work inside project scope and stop
elif safe_parallel_streams >= 2:
    dispatch one PowerShell lane orchestrator per safe stream
else:
    work the single stream or route the blocker
```

Acceptable reasons not to parallelize:
- file/hunk overlap;
- shared public contract unresolved;
- schema/migration ordering;
- architecture boundary unresolved;
- dirty worktree prevents safe isolation;
- resource constraints for heavy tests/builds;
- reviewer/user explicitly directed serial execution;
- only one graph-unblocked stream exists.

Unacceptable reasons:
- "simpler";
- "not necessary";
- "I can do it myself";
- "I forgot";
- "coordination overhead";
- "parallel agents might be overkill" without a concrete safety reason.

If parallelism is available but not dispatched, record:

```text
PARALLELISM EXCEPTION:
- streams considered:
- graph-unblocked tasks:
- reason parallelism is unsafe:
- conflicts:
- selected single stream:
- what would make parallelism safe:
```

Post this as a task comment or heartbeat when the workflow has a Kanban flight recorder and it is appropriate.

## Workstream Packet

Every subagent/workstream must receive a strict packet:

```text
WORKSTREAM PACKET

Board:
<board>

Workspace:
<workspace path>

Task:
<task_id> - <title containing [Codex], or body explicitly marked [Codex]>

Stream:
<stream name>

Goal:
<one concrete goal>

Project scope:
- <workspace/project root and any narrower allowed directories>

Allowed files/directories:
- <paths>

Forbidden files/directories:
- <paths>

Dependencies:
- Parents:
- Children:
- Stream terminal:
- Integration gates waiting on this:

Can run in parallel with:
- <streams/tasks>

Must serialize with:
- <streams/tasks and why>

Guardrails:
- Work only on this `[Codex]` ticket.
- Touch only files inside Project scope and Allowed files/directories.
- Do not edit sibling apps, parent repos, home-directory skill/config files, generated dependency folders, or external worktrees unless they are explicitly inside this ticket's Project scope.
- Finish exactly this task/slice.
- Do not bundle adjacent tickets.
- Do not mark Done; Hermes alone owns Done.
- Do not push unless explicitly authorized.
- Do not delete runtime/database data.
- Do not fabricate evidence, tests, outputs, product data, or runtime state.
- Test fixtures/mocks are allowed only when clearly test-only.
- If architecture/API/schema/security boundaries are unclear, do not cross them; report the exact boundary and continue safe same-card work inside the existing scope.

Verification:
- <narrowest relevant command>

Commit policy:
- Commit only intended files if the workflow requires commits.
- Do not stage unrelated files.
- Report dirty/untracked files separately.

Report format:
- summary:
- files changed:
- tests/checks run with exact output:
- commit SHA if any:
- blockers:
- risks:
- forbidden scope avoided:
- project scope respected:
- next unblocked task(s):
```

Coordinator responsibilities:
- choose launch batch;
- spawn one separate PowerShell lane orchestrator per safe independent lane;
- assign file ownership;
- prevent collisions;
- collect outputs;
- run or sequence heavy tests;
- integrate one stream at a time;
- post or prepare separate review handoffs;
- update Kanban comments/heartbeats as required.

For detailed subagent dispatch rules and a narrower DebateAI/Hermes-oriented template, see `references/subagents.md`.

## File Ownership and Conflict Policy

Before dispatching parallel workstreams, assign file ownership.

Rules:
- Two write workstreams must not edit the same file by default.
- If overlap is likely, serialize or make one workstream read-only.
- Shared config/schema/API files create a contract boundary; route or serialize.
- Docs-only streams can run beside code streams if files do not overlap.
- Read-only review, audit, and test-planning streams can run in parallel freely.
- Heavy builds/tests should be sequenced unless cheap and isolated.
- Re-check git status before and after integrating each workstream.
- Never stage unrelated/generated files.

Conflict template:

```text
FILE CONFLICT ROUTING:
- conflicting streams:
- conflicting files:
- chosen owner:
- deferred/serialized stream:
- reason:
- next safe action:
```

## Review and Done Ownership

Hermes exclusively owns Done:
- Codex never marks Done and never calls the Kanban completion command.
- Codex submits a review handoff only.
- Hermes approves and marks Done, or blocks/returns the same card.
- A Hermes block immediately sends only that same card back to In Progress for Codex rework.
- Codex may continue independent safe streams while waiting only when the board and resource law authorize them.

General handoff:

```text
READY FOR REVIEW:
- task:
- stream:
- summary:
- files changed:
- commit SHA:
- tests/checks:
  - command:
  - exact output:
- subagents/workstreams used:
- risks/blockers:
- fake evidence/data check:
- dirty/untracked files:
- next believed unblocked task(s):
```

If the project requires exact marker text, use that exact marker. For Hermes-style review, use:

```text
READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:
```

Parallel review rules:
- Separate tasks get separate handoffs.
- Do not collapse unrelated streams into one mega-handoff.
- Reviewers can review multiple independent tickets if handoffs are clean.
- Children/gates should not start until parents are approved unless the graph/workflow explicitly allows it.

Use `references/packet-formats.md` when exact READY/BLOCKED/STALLED/HEARTBEAT packet formats are required.

## Autonomous Handoff and Wait Loop

After launch, keep the recurring Kanban watch loop alive. After completing a wave, do not exit, do not wait for the user manually, and do not treat chat as the source of truth. Handoff means continue the Kanban polling/await loop.

When a wave is finished:

1. For each completed ticket, post a Kanban comment beginning exactly:

```text
READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:
```

2. Include for each ticket:
   - ticket ID/title;
   - files changed;
   - exact tests/checks run;
   - commit SHA;
   - risks/blockers;
   - subagents/workstreams used;
   - next believed unblocked ticket.
3. Do not mark tickets Done. Hermes owns Done; Codex owns returning Blocked cards to In Progress for rework.
4. After posting `READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:`, enter the polling loop.

Polling loop:
- Poll once immediately after heartbeat-protocol launch, then every 1 minute while the session remains active unless current V/Hermes authority specifies another cadence.
- After each handoff, blocker, Hermes approval, Hermes return, or dependency-stream transition, poll immediately, then continue the 1-minute cadence.
- Inspect all in-scope `[Codex]` Ready/Running/Review/For-review/Blocked tickets and recent comments, including the same tickets handed off.
- Look specifically for Hermes review results:
  - ticket status changed to done;
  - `HERMES REVIEW BLOCKER` comment;
  - `HERMES ROUTING HOLD` comment;
  - new Ready child/promoted task;
  - explicit Hermes routing comment.

