0004: ---
0005: 
0006: # Heartbeat Protocol
0007: 
0008: ## Overview
0009: 
0010: Use this protocol when Codex is acting as an implementation coordinator for a Kanban-backed goal.
0011: 
0012: Core doctrine:
0013: - Kanban is source of truth.
0014: - For DebateAI work, Hermes is the cockpit broker by default when V invokes heartbeat-protocol.
0015: - Codex may only implement tickets explicitly marked `[Codex]`.
0016: - Codex may only touch files inside the declared project/workspace scope for the active `[Codex]` ticket.
0017: - A Blocked `[Codex]` card is active rework, not parked work. Immediately read the blocker, transition only that same card from `Blocked` to `In Progress` (native Hermes status `running`), and resume the same worker or dispatch one scoped replacement continuity.
0018: - If Kanban has no Ready, Running, or Blocked `[Codex]` implementation tickets inside the declared project scope, Codex must tell V/user that no Kanban-backed Codex work is available and stop. Do not substitute pasted plans, PRDs, chat memory, or inferred lanes for missing Kanban tickets.
0019: - Do not rely on chat memory or an old plan when Kanban state is available.
0020: - Dependency streams are execution lanes.
0021: - Parallel lanes are the default when safe, but worktrees are not proof of independence.
0022: - Each truly independent implementation lane/module gets its own git worktree before implementation begins.
0023: - Subagents/workstreams are mandatory when graph-unblocked work can be split safely.
0024: - The coordinator coordinates; subagents/workstreams execute scoped slices.
0025: - Review and Done ownership must be explicit.
0026: - No fake evidence. No fake runtime data.
0027: - Once launched, heartbeat-protocol runs a recurring Kanban watch loop: poll immediately, then every 1 minute while active unless current V/Hermes authority specifies another cadence.
0028: 
0029: The default Hermes loop is mandatory:
0030: 
0031: ```text
0032: Hermes orders -> Codex activates the card -> scoped worker/subagent works
0033: -> Codex verifies and posts READY FOR HERMES REVIEW
0034: -> Hermes approves and marks Done, or Hermes blocks/returns the same card
0035: -> Codex immediately returns that card to In Progress and keeps working
0036: ```
0037: 
0038: Blocked is a feedback state, never a reason by itself to idle, end the goal, or ask V to restart Codex.
0039: 
0040: Roles:
0041: - Coordinator: read the board, map dependency streams, assign workstreams, prevent file conflicts, verify outputs, integrate work, and maintain Kanban handoffs/heartbeats.
0042: - Lane orchestrator agent: one separate PowerShell-launched agent/session per safe independent `[Codex]` lane. It owns orchestration for that lane, launches/continues the lane's coding subagent(s), reviews their outputs before Hermes handoff, and keeps polling that lane after handoff.
0043: - Subagents/workstreams: execute one scoped ticket or lane slice, respect allowed/forbidden files, run narrow verification, and report real outputs.
0044: - Coding subagent: a lane-local implementation agent assigned to a specific ticket/slice. If Hermes sends that ticket back, the same coding subagent/session must make the fix when technically possible; if the original session cannot be resumed, the lane orchestrator must explicitly reconstruct the same ownership context and report the continuity break.
0045: - Hermes reviewer: review diffs/tests, approve or block, and exclusively own Done. A block returns the same card to Codex rework immediately.
0046: 
0047: This skill is project-agnostic. Project-specific commands, boards, tenants, reviewers, and review markers must be inferred from the prompt, repo, Kanban metadata, or user-provided dependency streams. Hermes examples are examples only.
