0006: 
0007: # Playwright CLI Skill
0008: 
0009: Drive a real browser from the terminal using `playwright-cli`. Prefer the bundled wrapper script so the CLI works even when it is not globally installed.
0010: Treat this skill as CLI-first automation. Do not pivot to `@playwright/test` unless the user explicitly asks for test files.
0011: 
0012: ## Prerequisite check (required)
0013: 
0014: Before proposing commands, check whether `npx` is available (the wrapper depends on it):
0015: 
0016: ```bash
0017: command -v npx >/dev/null 2>&1
0018: ```
0019: 
0020: If it is not available, pause and ask the user to install Node.js/npm (which provides `npx`). Provide these steps verbatim:
0021: 
0022: ```bash
0023: # Verify Node/npm are installed
0024: node --version
0025: npm --version
0026: 
0027: # If missing, install Node.js/npm, then:
0028: npm install -g @playwright/cli@latest
0029: playwright-cli --help
0030: ```
0031: 
0032: Once `npx` is present, proceed with the wrapper script. A global install of `playwright-cli` is optional.
0033: 
0034: ## Skill path (set once)
0035: 
0036: ```bash
0037: export CODEX_HOME="${CODEX_HOME:-$HOME/.codex}"
0038: export PWCLI="$CODEX_HOME/skills/playwright/scripts/playwright_cli.sh"
0039: ```
0040: 
0041: User-scoped skills install under `$CODEX_HOME/skills` (default: `~/.codex/skills`).
0042: 
0043: ## Quick start
0044: 
0045: Use the wrapper script:
0046: 
0047: ```bash
0048: "$PWCLI" open https://playwright.dev --headed
0049: "$PWCLI" snapshot
0050: "$PWCLI" click e15
0051: "$PWCLI" type "Playwright"
0052: "$PWCLI" press Enter
0053: "$PWCLI" screenshot
0054: ```
0055: 
0056: If the user prefers a global install, this is also valid:
0057: 
0058: ```bash
0059: npm install -g @playwright/cli@latest
0060: playwright-cli --help
0061: ```
0062: 
0063: ## Core workflow
0064: 
0065: 1. Open the page.
0066: 2. Snapshot to get stable element refs.
0067: 3. Interact using refs from the latest snapshot.
0068: 4. Re-snapshot after navigation or significant DOM changes.
0069: 5. Capture artifacts (screenshot, pdf, traces) when useful.
0070: 
0071: Minimal loop:
0072: 
0073: ```bash
0074: "$PWCLI" open https://example.com
0075: "$PWCLI" snapshot
0076: "$PWCLI" click e3
0077: "$PWCLI" snapshot
0078: ```
0079: 
0080: ## When to snapshot again
0081: 
0082: Snapshot again after:
0083: 
0084: - navigation
0085: - clicking elements that change the UI substantially
0086: - opening/closing modals or menus
0087: - tab switches
0088: 
0089: Refs can go stale. When a command fails due to a missing ref, snapshot again.
0090: 
0091: ## Recommended patterns
0092: 
0093: ### Form fill and submit
0094: 
0095: ```bash
0096: "$PWCLI" open https://example.com/form
0097: "$PWCLI" snapshot
0098: "$PWCLI" fill e1 "user@example.com"
0099: "$PWCLI" fill e2 "password123"
0100: "$PWCLI" click e3
0101: "$PWCLI" snapshot
0102: ```
0103: 
0104: ### Debug a UI flow with traces
0105: 
0106: ```bash
0107: "$PWCLI" open https://example.com --headed
0108: "$PWCLI" tracing-start
0109: # ...interactions...
0110: "$PWCLI" tracing-stop
0111: ```
0112: 
0113: ### Multi-tab work
0114: 
0115: ```bash
0116: "$PWCLI" tab-new https://example.com
0117: "$PWCLI" tab-list
0118: "$PWCLI" tab-select 0
0119: "$PWCLI" snapshot
0120: ```
0121: 
0122: ## Wrapper script
0123: 
0124: The wrapper script uses `npx --package @playwright/cli playwright-cli` so the CLI can run without a global install:
0125: 
0126: ```bash
0127: "$PWCLI" --help
0128: ```
0129: 
0130: Prefer the wrapper unless the repository already standardizes on a global install.
0131: 
